create a new branch from main

# Start on main
git checkout main

# Create a new branch called 'feature1' from main
git checkout -b feature1

# Make changes, commit them
git add .
git commit -m "Add new feature"

# Push the branch to GitHub
git push -u origin feature1



how to initialize git on local

1) git init (This creates a .git folder inside your project — now your folder is a Git repository.)
2) git status (You’ll see your files listed as untracked.)
3) git add . (. means “add all files in this folder”.You can also add specific files: git add index.html script.js
)
4) git commit -m "Initial commit"
5) Create a repository on GitHub

Go to GitHub
 → log in.

Click New repository.

Enter a name (e.g., my-project), leave Initialize with README unchecked.

Click Create repository.

You’ll see a page with commands to connect your local repo.

6) Add the remote repository
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/my-project.git (origin is the default name for the remote)
7) If your local branch is main:

git branch -M main      # rename if needed
git push -u origin main
->-u sets upstream tracking so future pushes are simple:
->git push

8) git remote -v
origin  https://github.com/yourusername/my-project.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/yourusername/my-project.git (push)
